1. Patients may be impacted by the use of adjunctive hemodynamic indicators through better informed care decisions, as these devices provide real-time hemodynamic data that can help clinicians monitor patients' conditions and intervene when necessary.

2. Clear labeling and guidance on interpretation, as required by regulations (citing §870.2220), ensure patients understand the limitations of these devices. The labeling should include:
   - A clear description of what the device measures and outputs (§870.2220(5)(i));
   - Warnings identifying factors that may impact output results (§870.2220(5)(ii));
   - Guidance for interpretation of the outputs, including warning(s) specifying adjunctive use of the measurements (§870.2220(5)(iii));
   - Key assumptions made in the calculation and determination of measurements (§870.2220(5)(iv)); and
   - A summary of the clinical validation data, including details of the patient population studied, clinical study protocols, and device performance with confidence intervals for all intended use populations (§870.2220(5)(v)).

3. To ensure patients understand the device's limitations and adjunctive nature, healthcare providers should explain the labeling information in an understandable manner, discuss the device's intended use, explain the role of this device in managing the patient's condition, and address any concerns or questions the patients may have.

Relevant Source: Title 21 of the Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Part 870.2220 - Class II (Special controls) - Adjunctive hemodynamic indicator with decision point. (Accessed: 2022-04-01)
